name: census
after: 19-xargs
intro: Three programs that read a tree you did not make. Each spec pins the
intro: output exactly -- the shape is part of the requirement, not a detail --
intro: because your program is compared against a reference run on the same
intro: files, and two right answers have to agree on what right looks like.
intro:
intro: How you get there is still yours. Nothing here names a tool.
---
q: census.sh -- which severities a log is full of
spec: It takes one log file. Every line of that log is a date, a time, a
spec: severity and a message.
spec:
spec: It prints one line per severity that appears: the count, one space,
spec: then the severity. Most frequent first; a tie goes alphabetically.
spec:
spec: With anything other than exactly one argument it prints nothing and
spec: exits 2.
file: census.sh
prog: #!/bin/sh
prog: [ $# -eq 1 ] || exit 2
prog: awk '{ print $3 }' "$1" | sort | uniq -c | awk '{ print $1, $2 }' | sort -k1,1rn -k2,2
case: sh census.sh @log@
case: grep WARN @log@ > w.log; sh census.sh w.log
case: sh census.sh; echo $?
case: sh census.sh a b; echo $?
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: count them however you like -- the work is getting the severity out of each line and the two sort keys right
---
q: keep.sh -- trim a file to its tail, in place
spec: It takes a file and a number. Afterwards that file holds only its last
spec: N lines, in the order they were in.
spec:
spec: It prints nothing, and it leaves no other file behind it.
spec:
spec: With anything other than exactly two arguments it exits 2.
file: keep.sh
prog: #!/bin/sh
prog: [ $# -eq 2 ] || exit 2
prog: tail -n "$2" "$1" > "$1.keep" && mv "$1.keep" "$1"
case: sh keep.sh @txt@ 3; cat @txt@
case: sh keep.sh @txt@ 3; ls | wc -l
case: sh keep.sh @log@ 1; wc -l < @log@
case: sh keep.sh @txt@; echo $?
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: you cannot read a file and write to it at once -- the tail has to land somewhere before the original is replaced
---
q: sizes.sh -- how long every log in here is
spec: It takes no arguments. For every file in the current directory whose
spec: name ends in .log it prints the name, one space, then how many lines
spec: that file has.
spec:
spec: Sorted by name. A name with a space in it must still come out as one
spec: name and one count.
file: sizes.sh
prog: #!/bin/sh
prog: for f in *.log; do
prog: echo "$f $(wc -l < "$f")"
prog: done
case: sh sizes.sh
case: sh sizes.sh | wc -l
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: a glob hands you each name already split from the next -- what you do with the name after that is where the quoting matters
